Philipp Lucas wrote:

> id.fortytwo@gmail.com (Id_42) wrote:
> 
>>I've tried reading instructions from various threads, and I'm at a
>>loss. I'm running Windows 2000; can anyone (if it's not too much
>>trouble; if it is, forget it) give me _step-by-step_ instructions on
>>what I need to download, how I need to do it, etc? 
> 
> The necessary information is already in the installation documents of you
> tools. Nevertheless, here is a short overview.
> 
> (1) Make it so that you can compile NetHack.
>     (a) Get a compiler.
>         - I am familiar with DJGPP, a DOS compiler. You need the basic
>           compiler and the utility "make".
> 	- How to acquire the Borland compiler is described in the 
>           installation instructions for NetHack.
>         - You can download various version of Visual C from MS.
>     (b) Find out how you can compile and link simple programs. This
>         should be explained in the documentation of your compiler.
>     (c) Follow the instructions in sys/winnt/Install.nt to set up 
>         NetHack. You can adjust various features (wizard mode, Elbereth)
>         and set some parameters (e. g., number of elements of the record
>         list).
>     (d) Compile NetHack, as per instructions in sys/winnt/Install.nt. 
>         Usual, this goes through flawlessly, if you have done step (c) 
>         correctly.
> (2) Make it so that so can patch NetHack.
>     (a) Get a patch utility.
>         - DJGPP offers one.
>         - Cygwin also.
>     (b) Acquire necessary patches.
>     (c) For each patch:
>         (i) Go to the topmost directory in the NetHack source tree.
>         (ii) type "patch -p1 < NAME_OF_PATCH". You get information
>              about the progress of the patching. Some patches may fail,
>              in particular if you have previously applied other patches.
>              Most of the problems can be dealt with easily, if you know
>              C and something about the NetHack source. Otherwise, ask.
>         (iii) Recompile and test.
> 
> If you have problems with (1.a) or (1.b), consult the specific newsgroup
> for the compiler of your choice. For everything else, ask here. The most
> likely opportunities for problems and questions are (1.c) and (2.c.ii). 

I would change the command in 2.c.ii from "patch -p1 < NAME_OF_PATCH" to 
"patch -lp1 < NAME_OF_PATCH".

Reason being that patches are created in so many different environments 
that you never know what that whitespace is.

